 the bad times and a lot of  people told me it was but I said no I  think we will get the show back on the  road I think we will be able to improve  people's living standards in the years  ahead and then along came the euro  crisis the war in Ukraine the war in  Syria the Ebola epidemic you know the  world everywhere I went in the last  decade there was always a reason for  somebody to say to me you can't still be  a rational optimist given what's  happening X around the world and yet as  I say this has been an extraordinary  decade there's a really beautiful  experiment that Hans Rosling did whose  fellow optimist sadly who died but who  was one of the pioneers of this kind of  thinking swedish health economists and  what he did was he asked a thousand  people in the last 20 years has the  percentage of the world population that  lives in extreme poverty that's a dollar  ninety a day or less doubled half or  stayed the same in those 20 years and in  the u.s. sixty-five percent of people  said it has doubled five percent said it  had hard the five percent were right the  65 percent were wrong that's pretty  striking  but what Rosling then did was he said if  I had written those three answers on  three bananas and thrown the three  bananas into a cage with a chimpanzee in  it the chimpanzee would have picked up  the right answer 33 percent of the time  not 5 percent of the time it would have  done six times as well as human beings  in answering a question about human  society so what's the problem here the  problem is not that we don't know what's  going on in the world it's that we know  things that are not true we're  absolutely convinced of things that  actually are not true  and that seems to me a growing problem  that largely because of media coverage  of negative events which is always  happened but is becoming more extreme  all the time that's because of that we  end up with a strong impression that  things are going wrong in the world  and we don't know that 